      "note": "The finite-difference figure is the best point of a step-size sweep, not a single arbitrary step. Central differences trade truncation error (falling as eps^2) against roundoff amplification (growing as 1/eps, because the quotient divides the forward solve's own convergence error by the step), so the per-config sweep in fd_sweep_rel_diff shows that U-shape explicitly. A single small step would report a large discrepancy that is a property of the oracle, not of the gradient. The cvxpylayers comparison carries the real weight regardless, since it shares no code path with the closed-form tilt and differentiates the KKT system of the general cone program instead."
